The "trivial/non-trivial" framing came directly from user corrections
in a live session. The plan draft had "stop before every --continue",
and the user pushed back: "stop at non-trivial conflicts, that's all."
The AUTO_MERGE --stat rule came from the same session: "always surface
it, approval or not."

The two bullet points deliberately don't enumerate every possible
non-trivial case — the list would be both long and incomplete. The
common thread is "unexpected state": anything that doesn't fit the
pattern of "rerere resolved it and the build agrees" is non-trivial.

Placement is immediately after the in-session pause paragraph because
the two rules are complementary: one covers explicit stop instructions,
the other covers the autonomous default when no instruction is in
effect.
